{
 "id": "2026-liu-public",
 "name": "Public Art and Restorative Streets",
 "description": "Does a piece of public art make a shopping street more restorative, and does its design matter? Participants see seven versions of one scene on Tanhualin commercial pedestrian street in Wuhan: the street as it is, and the street with a bench-and-shade art piece in six designs (abstract or figurative form; theme Zeng Houyi's chime bells, the phoenix, or the Yellow Crane Tower). Imagining they are tired after a busy week and strolling here in their free time, they rate each scene on the 11 items of the Perceived Restorativeness Scale (PRS-11: being away, fascination, coherence, scope), 0 = not at all, 6 = rather much, 10 = completely. Scene order is random and each scene stays on screen for at least 30 s. Demographics as in the paper: gender, age, education, emotional impact over the past month, more than two years in Wuhan. Rebuilt from Liu & Zhou (2026), Buildings 16(3): 593 (n = 195), with the paper's CC BY images. Differences from the original study: (1) English instead of the Chinese PRS-11 by Li et al.; item wording is the English PRS-11 as listed by Kim et al. (2022, IJERPH 19: 10231), with 'that place' / 'places like that' written as 'places like this'; (2) the no-art scene is the paper's Fig 3 scaled down to the size of the Fig 5 panels; its colour and sharpness may differ slightly from the six composites; (3) the three practice items before the main task are not included; (4) the paper used the emotional-impact item to screen out participants; here it is only recorded; (5) seconds spent per scene are also recorded.",
